WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.822
The last lesson pulls several circuit ideas together, which is exactly why it can blur if the learner stops naming quantities carefully.

2
00:00:05.822 --> 00:00:11.643
Charge is what moves around the loop.

3
00:00:11.643 --> 00:00:17.465
Voltage is the energy transferred per coulomb by the source.

4
00:00:17.465 --> 00:00:23.287
Resistance belongs to the route and helps set the current.

5
00:00:23.287 --> 00:00:29.108
Power tells how quickly energy is transferred.

6
00:00:29.108 --> 00:00:34.930
A clean capstone answer keeps those quantities separate and then links them in the right order.

7
00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:40.752
That is how the same source can produce different currents and powers in different loops without changing its voltage label.

8
00:00:40.752 --> 00:00:46.573
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 becomes safer once the learner treats each electrical quantity as a separate ledger entry with its own job.

9
00:00:46.573 --> 00:00:52.395
Charge means what moves.

10
00:00:52.395 --> 00:00:58.217
Charge carriers circulate around the loop rather than being used up by the components.

11
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:04.038
Voltage and resistance means what sets the current.

12
00:01:04.038 --> 00:01:09.860
The source provides the push per charge, while the route opposition controls how much current results.

13
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:15.956
This diagram matters because it keeps every quantity on its own line.

14
00:01:15.956 --> 00:01:22.051
The charge carriers circulate around the full loop.

15
00:01:22.051 --> 00:01:28.147
The source gives each carrier an energy boost measured by voltage.

16
00:01:28.147 --> 00:01:34.242
The route provides the resistance that limits how much current actually flows.

17
00:01:34.242 --> 00:01:40.338
And power is then read as the rate of energy transfer once the current and voltage are known.

18
00:01:40.338 --> 00:01:46.433
The board prevents the learner from letting one number stand in for the whole circuit story.

19
00:01:46.433 --> 00:01:52.529
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong capstone explanation starts by keeping the electrical quantities separate before linking them together.

20
00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:58.624
Moving quantity: Charge is the quantity carried around the loop by the carriers.

21
00:01:58.624 --> 00:02:04.720
Control quantities: Voltage belongs to the source boost and resistance belongs to the route.

22
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:10.469
A delivery-route analogy helps because it gives each circuit quantity a job.

23
00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:16.218
The vehicles are like moving charge carriers.

24
00:02:16.218 --> 00:02:21.967
The depot gives each vehicle fuel for the trip, which mirrors the energy-per-charge role of voltage.

25
00:02:21.967 --> 00:02:27.716
Narrow or rough roads act like resistance because they reduce how easily the vehicles can move around the route.

26
00:02:27.716 --> 00:02:33.465
The number of trips per minute is like current, and the total work done each minute is like power.

27
00:02:33.465 --> 00:02:39.215
The analogy matters because it trains the learner to separate the quantities before relating them.

28
00:02:39.215 --> 00:02:44.964
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy helps the learner keep the capstone quantities distinct before the calculations begin.

29
00:02:44.964 --> 00:02:50.713
Vehicles means moving charge.

30
00:02:50.713 --> 00:02:56.462
Something moves around the route and keeps circulating through the system.

31
00:02:56.462 --> 00:03:02.211
Fuel and road means voltage and resistance.

32
00:03:02.211 --> 00:03:07.960
The supplied boost and the route difficulty are different controls on the outcome.

33
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:13.449
The capstone uses a small set of connected formulas.

34
00:03:13.449 --> 00:03:18.938
Ohm's law tells you the current from the source voltage and the route resistance.

35
00:03:18.938 --> 00:03:24.428
Power can then be found from power equals current times voltage.

36
00:03:24.428 --> 00:03:29.917
The important thing is not to let these formulas erase the meaning of the quantities.

37
00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:35.406
Voltage still means energy per charge.

38
00:03:35.406 --> 00:03:40.895
Resistance still belongs to the route.

39
00:03:40.895 --> 00:03:46.385
Current is still the flow rate that results, and power is still the rate of energy transfer produced by that current and voltage together.

40
00:03:46.385 --> 00:03:51.874
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene becomes trustworthy once the learner can explain why the same source can produce different currents and powers in different loops.

41
00:03:51.874 --> 00:03:57.363
Current step: Use the same source voltage with each route resistance to find the resulting current.

42
00:03:57.363 --> 00:04:02.852
Power step: Once the current is known, combine it with the voltage to find the energy transfer rate.

43
00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:08.342
I = V / R.

44
00:04:08.342 --> 00:04:13.831
P = I V.

45
00:04:13.831 --> 00:04:19.320
Keep the ledger clean: calculate the current first, then use it with the voltage to find the power.

46
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:25.187
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

47
00:04:25.187 --> 00:04:31.054
First name the quantities and say which one belongs to the source, which belongs to the route, and which one is being asked for.

48
00:04:31.054 --> 00:04:36.921
Second link them with the right formulas by finding current before power if needed.

49
00:04:36.921 --> 00:04:42.788
Third summarize what stayed the same and what changed across the compared circuits.

50
00:04:42.788 --> 00:04:48.655
This keeps the capstone question organized and protects the learner from writing one number as though it explains the whole loop.

51
00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:54.522
Keep this takeaway in view: Name, link, and summarize is the safest routine for every capstone circuit comparison.

52
00:04:54.522 --> 00:05:00.389
Step 1 means name the quantity roles.

53
00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:06.256
Separate moving charge, source voltage, route resistance, current, and power before calculation.

54
00:05:06.256 --> 00:05:12.123
Step 2 means link them in order.

55
00:05:12.123 --> 00:05:17.990
Use voltage and resistance to find current, then use current with voltage to find power.

56
00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:23.814
A common weak sentence says the battery gives the same current no matter what it is connected to.

57
00:05:23.814 --> 00:05:29.638
This is exactly what the capstone must correct.

58
00:05:29.638 --> 00:05:35.462
The battery may provide the same voltage across two different circuits, but if the route resistance changes then the resulting current changes as well.

59
00:05:35.462 --> 00:05:41.286
That is why two loops on the same source can have different current and power values.

60
00:05:41.286 --> 00:05:47.110
The source label alone is not the whole circuit story.

61
00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:52.934
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 becomes safer once the learner stops treating a battery voltage label as though it were a fixed current promise.

62
00:05:52.934 --> 00:05:58.758
Same source means same voltage.

63
00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:04.582
The battery can keep its voltage while different routes produce different outcomes.

64
00:06:04.582 --> 00:06:10.406
Different routes means different current.

65
00:06:10.406 --> 00:06:16.230
Changing the resistance changes how much current the loop can sustain.

66
00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:22.179
Power is not another name for current, and it is not another name for voltage either.

67
00:06:22.179 --> 00:06:28.128
It tells you how quickly energy is being transferred in the circuit.

68
00:06:28.128 --> 00:06:34.077
That is why a higher current at the same voltage produces a larger power value.

69
00:06:34.077 --> 00:06:40.026
In the capstone example, reducing the resistance increases the current, and that in turn increases the power.

70
00:06:40.026 --> 00:06:45.975
Keeping power on its own line makes the final comparison much more meaningful.

71
00:06:45.975 --> 00:06:51.924
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 stays rich when power is treated as the circuit's output-rate line, not as a renamed current.

72
00:06:51.924 --> 00:06:57.873
Power meaning means energy transfer rate.

73
00:06:57.873 --> 00:07:03.822
Power tells how quickly energy is delivered or transferred in the circuit.

74
00:07:03.822 --> 00:07:09.771
Same V, more I means more power.

75
00:07:09.771 --> 00:07:15.720
At fixed voltage, a larger current produces a larger power value.

76
00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.048
Run four checks.

77
00:07:20.048 --> 00:07:24.376
Charge is what moves.

78
00:07:24.376 --> 00:07:28.705
Voltage is energy per charge from the source.

79
00:07:28.705 --> 00:07:33.033
Resistance belongs to the route and helps set the current.

80
00:07:33.033 --> 00:07:37.361
And power tells how quickly energy is transferred once current and voltage are known.

81
00:07:37.361 --> 00:07:41.689
Those four checks capture the capstone structure before the arithmetic begins.

82
00:07:41.689 --> 00:07:46.017
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can keep the circuit quantities separate while linking them in order.

83
00:07:46.017 --> 00:07:50.345
Charge means what moves.

84
00:07:50.345 --> 00:07:54.674
The carriers circulate around the loop and are not used up.

85
00:07:54.674 --> 00:07:59.002
Voltage means energy per charge.

86
00:07:59.002 --> 00:08:03.330
The source provides the per-coulomb boost, not a guaranteed current.

87
00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:09.345
Start by writing down what the source gives and what the route changes.

88
00:08:09.345 --> 00:08:15.361
Then use Ohm's law to find the current in each case.

89
00:08:15.361 --> 00:08:21.376
After that, use power equals current times voltage to compare the output rates.

90
00:08:21.376 --> 00:08:27.392
Finish by saying explicitly what stayed the same and what changed across the two circuits.

91
00:08:27.392 --> 00:08:33.407
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

92
00:08:33.407 --> 00:08:39.423
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 6 becomes much safer once the learner can explain a same-source, different-loop comparison without merging the electrical quantities.

93
00:08:39.423 --> 00:08:45.438
Do not skip the quantity labels: Say which number is voltage, which is resistance, which is current, and which is power.

94
00:08:45.438 --> 00:08:51.454
Do not skip the same-source check: If the voltage stayed the same, say so before explaining why the current changed.

95
00:08:51.454 --> 00:08:57.469
name the source voltage and the route resistance.

96
00:08:57.469 --> 00:09:03.485
find the current in each case using Ohm's law.

97
00:09:03.485 --> 00:09:09.500
The best answers finish with a clean summary of what stayed fixed and what changed.
